import "core"


# The three things a print golden can be, for the test engine's own tests ([catln test] derives
  one test per print that renders to text). The first carries the text its value renders to and
  passes. The second carries other text and fails, naming both. The third carries no golden at
  all and fails, saying to run `catln test --update`.
  Written as a page of its own rather than in the integration fixtures: a fixture is checked by
  what its [main] does, and what is checked here is the engine's verdict on each print, which only
  a test of the engine can read.
> "matches its golden"
  >>> matches its golden
> "differs from its golden"
  >>> some other text
> "has no golden"
main{io -> IO} -> (){IO} =
  _<- exit(val= 0)
  ()
